// Labelled query pairs for semantic-cache threshold calibration.
//
// "equivalent": true means an answer to query_a is a CORRECT AND COMPLETE answer
// to query_b. That is a stronger claim than "these are similar" -- and the gap
// between the two is what the threshold has to learn. Roughly half the negatives
// below are deliberately near-identical strings with different correct answers;
// a threshold tuned on hit rate alone serves those and returns a wrong answer.
//
// PROVENANCE: these labels are editorial judgements made while building this
// project, not crowd-sourced annotations, and not a sample of production
// traffic. They are a reasonable proxy for calibrating the mechanism. They are
// not evidence about any real workload's repeat-query distribution.
//
// HOW BIG DOES THIS NEED TO BE? Bounded by the rule of three, not by taste. With
// zero observed false hits in n served responses the 95% upper bound is about
// 3/n, so the tightest ceiling you can DEFEND is capped by how many responses
// the threshold serves -- no matter how good the threshold is:
//
//     served    20     50    100    200    300    500   1000
//     bound  16.1%  7.1%   3.7%   1.9%   1.3%   0.8%   0.4%
//
// At the ~25% hit rate these thresholds produce, a 1% ceiling therefore needs
// roughly 1200 labelled pairs. This file has ~200, which supports a ~4% ceiling.
// That is the honest state of this layer.

